// `forge company run <company-dir> <operator-file>` (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-27-forge-company-layer-design.md, sections 4.3,
// 7 and 10): run the company's steps in dependency order until it is done or something stops it. The decisions are pure and live
// elsewhere (`company_run.next_action`, `company_step`); this module is the loop that acts on them, and the only part of the company
// layer that starts a process.
//
// The loop. It takes the writer lock of the company directory (`<dir>/lock`, the one `decide` takes, so a decision is never written
// in the middle of a run), reads the manifest, the operator file and the price table once, and then, round after round: reads
// company.journal and REPLAYS it (a journal the replay refuses is refused as `status` refuses it, and nothing is written; a torn tail
// is cut), derives the spent cost from it (`company_state.spent_cost_micro`: the recorded cost, never re-priced), asks
// `next_action`, and does that one thing. Nothing the loop knows is carried from one round to the next but what the journal says:
// a step is done when the replay says it is accepted, never because a flag in memory says so.
//
// A step (`Start(i)`):
//   1. Refusals that write nothing: a role no worker runs (none since task 11: `task-type-unsupported`), a worker step with no `check`
//      or a product step with neither a gate nor a check (`check-missing`: a worker's claim is never acceptance), a missing
//      `steps/<id>/ev` (`step-dir-missing`: Forge cannot make a directory; the operator makes `steps/<id>/ev` for each worker step),
//      an operator file without what the step needs (`operator-invalid`).
//   1b. Needs (task 8c; spec section 10, "`needs` passes the accepted artifact's path and its sha256"). The round that answers
//      `Start(i)` writes, from the replayed journal, the list of what the step needs (`company_step.needs_text`: each need's
//      recorded path and SHA-256, `company_state.artifact_of`; a need run by a PERSON, a product step, is left out: its
//      "artifact" is the whole manifest, which this run has just parsed and trusts, so byte-verifying it would block every
//      dependent on any later edit of company.json, with nothing to give). Before the step is admitted (`take_needs`), EVERY
//      need's artifact is read again by `read_artifact`, the very rules it was hashed by at acceptance, and must be that step's
//      artifact with that digest; otherwise `artifact-invalid` with `reason: needs-missing` or `needs-changed`, exit 14, nothing
//      admitted, written or removed (all are checked before the first write), so a dependent step is never given anything but
//      bytes that were checked. A builder or writer step is then GIVEN the bytes
//      just checked, in its execution directory (the build tool's working directory, the only one it is sure to read): a copy
//      `needs-<need id>-<artifact name>` of each and `needs.json`, the list (`[{"step","artifact","path","sha256"}]`, `path` the
//      copy's name), which the brief can point the builder at. Flat names in the directory the operator already made, because
//      Forge cannot make a directory (no `mkdir`); so no new operator duty. Each file is written as `needs.tmp`, created with
//      O_EXCL and O_NOFOLLOW beneath the directory, and renamed over its name: a link the previous build left at that name is
//      replaced, never written through; the mode is 0644 less the umask, never group- or world-writable. Every start first
//      removes the `needs.json` and `needs-*` an earlier start left (the names are Forge's in a builder's execution directory)
//      and writes them again, so a resumed or retried step, or one whose needs changed, gets exactly its current ones. A qa,
//      reviewer or product step is verified the same way and given nothing: what it runs over is the operator's (a checkout) or
//      the manifest.
//   1b'. The contract (task 10). A builder or writer step may name a `contract`, a file of the company directory holding the exact
//      public signatures the build must use. After the needs, `take_contract` reads it (`company_files.read_artifact`, the rules of
//      an artifact) and writes Forge's own copy `contract.txt` in the execution directory (`company_files.deliver_contract`); a file
//      that is not there or not readable is `artifact-invalid` (`reason: contract-missing|contract-unreadable`), exit 14, the step not
//      admitted. The build is started with `contract.txt` as its contract (the adapter passes `--contract <execution dir>/contract.txt`).
//      When the build ends, before the check, the copy is read again and compared with the company directory's file
//      (`company_files.contract_intact`): a build that changed its contract is held (`reason: contract-changed`). A step with no
//      contract is started as before. No new authority: the rows are the needs delivery's.
//   1c. Stale workers (task 9). Before a builder step is admitted, a build an earlier, killed run started in `steps/<id>/ev` is
//      asked to stop through its worker and settled in the step's workspace (`reap_stale`; `reaped: <task>` is printed), so the
//      next build never meets a supervisor that still holds the execution directory, and nothing of the killed run keeps going.
//   2. `company.step.admitted` (forge_task: the step's Forge workspace, `steps/<id>`). A step admitted and never accepted (a run
//      killed in the middle of it, a check that failed) is admitted again by the next run: the count of admissions is the record.
//      The operator file must not be inside the company directory (`company_step.inside`, `operator-invalid`).
//   3. The worker, through the very runs `forge build` and `forge gate` are (`step_run.run_build` / `run_gate`, which print as those
//      verbs do): a builder or writer step builds the step's `input` (decoded, written to `steps/<id>/brief.txt`) in `steps/<id>/ev`
//      with the workspace `steps/<id>`; a qa or reviewer step reports the gate of the repository, revision and checkout the operator
//      file gives for the step id; an analyst step (task 11, `tool_step`) runs the tool, argv and input the operator file gives for
//      the step id through the tool runner (`tool_call.run_step`), its artifact the tool's kept standard output `tool.out`. They take the lock of `steps/<id>/lock`, a different file from the company's lock, so holding
//      the company's lock across them does not deadlock. A product step has no worker.
//   4. A build's usage: `steps/<id>/ev/usage.jsonl` priced call by call (`company_budget.parse_usage_for`, the over tier per call),
//      less what the journal has already recorded for the step, is appended as `company.spend.recorded` with its `cost_micro`,
//      whatever the build's ending (tokens spent on a build that got stuck are spent).
//   5. A worker that ended without a claim (`forge build` / `forge gate` exit status not 0) stops the run: `step-failed`, no check.
//   6. The check: Forge hashes the artifact (`steps/<id>/ev/project.cho` for a build, `report.json` for a gate, `company.json` for a
//      product step) opened BENEATH its directory with no link followed and only if it is a regular file (`hash_artifact`: anything
//      else is `artifact-invalid`, `company_step.rule_artifact_invalid`), runs the step's `check` program from the company directory with the artifact's path and the execution
//      directory as its two arguments, `PATH` as its whole environment, a deadline and an output cap, and hashes the artifact again.
//      The verdict (`company_step.check_verdict`): pass -> `company.step.accepted` with the artifact's path (relative to the company
//      directory) and the SHA-256 Forge computed, and the next round; a score below its total -> `company.step.check_failed` and
//      the run ends `check-failed`; anything else -> `company.step.check_unavailable` and `check-unavailable` (or `artifact-invalid`
//      for an artifact that is missing, not a regular file, too large, or changed while the check ran).
//   The check runs under its guard (workers/check_guard.cho, `run_check`): the guard, in a session of its own, starts the check in
//   another and kills its group at the deadline, past the output cap, or as soon as it can take `<dir>/check.lock`, which Forge
//   holds while it waits: so a Forge that is killed, however, leaves no check running. The operator's workers directory holds
//   `check_guard` beside `cancho_code` and `gate_runner`; without it every check is unavailable.
//   No Forge-level retry (spec section 10): a check_failed or check_unavailable record HOLDS the step (`company_state` header);
//   `next_action` answers `Held` and the run prints the refusal again, with the `retry:` line, and exits 13 or 14 without starting
//   or spending anything, until a person approves `retry:<id>` with `forge company decide`.
//
// Spend that lags (docs/architecture.md section 7, `budget-exhausted`): a run killed after a build and before its spend record has
// spent tokens the journal does not hold until that step runs again; a `usage.jsonl` rewritten shorter loses its calls up to the old
// total (the delta is never negative).
//   A product step without a check is accepted on its approved gate alone: the human decision recorded in the journal is its
//   check (section 4.2); its artifact is the manifest.
//
// A step's worker saying `done` therefore never writes `accepted`: only step 6 does, and only on a pass of Forge's own run of the
// check against bytes Forge hashed (spec section 7, the lying-worker test in tests/conformance/company_run.sh).
//
// Exit status and the printed refusals: the head of src/company_step.cho. Everything is printed on standard output, except what the
// build and gate runs print on standard error as `forge build` and `forge gate` do.
//
// Kill and resume (task 9; docs/architecture.md section 4.4.2; tests/conformance/company_kill.sh). TERM and INT are claimed while
// a build runs (`worker_run`, `signals("INT,TERM")`, passed down from main): a signal then cancels the build through the adapter
// (`await_run.finish_or_stop`: the supervisor kills the build's group; a signal during `execute` itself, before `accepted`, is
// cancelled by the request id, `await_run.cancel_unaccepted` in `build_run.run`), the spend is recorded, `stopped: signal` is printed and the
// run exits 16; the step stays admitted and not accepted. At any other moment, and for SIGKILL always, Forge ends the default way:
// a check's guard then kills the check; a build's supervisor and its build run on (they are meant to outlive a Forge, for `forge
// await`) until the next run reaps them (1c). The lock is a flock, so the next run is never refused by a dead one.
//
// Authority: the forge binary's, plus `signals("INT,TERM")` and `signals_read` (task 9), `exec_detached` (task 8b) and `dir_write` (task 8c: the needs written beneath the execution
// directory, `put_beneath`; `fs_write("")` writes through a link, `dir_open_new`/`dir_rename` do not): the check is started with `exec_spawn_detached_under` beneath the company
// directory, so it leads a process group of its own and the deadline's kill reaches whatever it started. The program is a name in
// the company directory (`exec_under`, no path in the report); the journal, the brief and the lock are written with the
// `fs_write`/`file_write` the binary already holds.

fn check_timeout_default() -> [] int {
    return 600000;
}

fn rule_step_dir_missing() -> [] &static [byte] {
    return "step-dir-missing";
}

fn rule_step_failed() -> [] &static [byte] {
    return "step-failed";
}

fn rule_operator_invalid() -> [] &static [byte] {
    return "operator-invalid";
}

fn rule_gate_open() -> [] &static [byte] {
    return "gate-open";
}

fn rule_gate_rejected() -> [] &static [byte] {
    return "gate-rejected";
}

fn say[&i, &l, &v](io: &!i Io, label: &l [byte], value: &v [byte]) -> [io_write] int {
    return company_cmd.line(io, label, value);
}

fn refuse[&i, &t](io: &!i Io, tag: &t [byte]) -> [io_write] int {
    return company_cmd.refuse(io, tag);
}

fn say_nat[&h, &i, &l](heap: &!h Heap, io: &!i Io, label: &l [byte], n: int) -> [heap, io_write] int {
    var b = buffer.push_nat(heap, buffer.empty(heap, 8), n);
    borrow b as &bb in {
        say(io, label, buffer.bytes(bb));
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, b);
    return 0;
}

fn glue[&h, &a, &b](heap: &!h Heap, first: &a [byte], second: &b [byte]) -> [heap] buffer.Buffer {
    return buffer.append(heap, buffer.append(heap, buffer.empty(heap, 64), first), second);
}

// ---- the verb --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `forge company run <company-dir> <operator-file>`: args are `forge company run <dir> <operator-file>`.
pub fn run_cmd[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &g, &z](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, args: &g Args, sig: &z Signals("INT,TERM")) -> [args, heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_read, file_write, io_write, err_write, signals("INT,TERM"), signals_read] int {
    if arg_count(args) != 5 {
        return company_step.exit_usage();
    }
    let dir = arg(args, 3);
    let opath = arg(args, 4);
    let mpath = company_cmd.under(heap, dir, "company.json");
    let jpath = company_cmd.under(heap, dir, "company.journal");
    let lpath = company_cmd.under(heap, dir, "lock");
    var code = company_step.exit_refused();
    borrow mpath as &mp in {
        borrow jpath as &jp in {
            borrow lpath as &lp in {
                if journal_io.path_ok(fs, buffer.bytes(mp)) == false || journal_io.path_ok(fs, buffer.bytes(jp)) == false || journal_io.path_ok(fs, buffer.bytes(lp)) == false || journal_io.path_ok(fs, opath) == false {
                    refuse(io, task.rule_name(task.Rule::PathInvalid));
                    code = company_step.exit_usage();
                } else if company_step.inside(dir, opath) {
                    refuse(io, rule_operator_invalid());
                    say(io, "reason: the operator file is inside the company directory", "");
                    code = company_step.exit_refused();
                } else {
                    match lock.acquire(fs, buffer.bytes(lp)) {
                        lock.Lock::Refused(rule) => {
                            refuse(io, task.rule_code_name(rule));
                            code = company_step.exit_refused();
                        }
                        lock.Lock::Failed(errno) => {
                            say(io, "error: cannot take the lock of the company directory", "");
                            code = company_step.exit_io();
                        }
                        lock.Lock::Held(held) => {
                            code = run_locked(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, dir, buffer.bytes(mp), buffer.bytes(jp), opath, sig);
                            lock.let_go(held);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, mpath);
    buffer.drop(heap, jpath);
    buffer.drop(heap, lpath);
    return code;
}

// The lock is held: the manifest.
fn run_locked[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &d, &m, &j, &o, &z](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, dir: &d [byte], mpath: &m [byte], jpath: &j [byte], opath: &o [byte], sig: &z Signals("INT,TERM")) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_read, file_write, io_write, err_write, signals("INT,TERM"), signals_read] int {
    let (text, errno) = company_cmd.read_limited(heap, fs, mpath, company_manifest.max_bytes() + 1);
    var code = company_step.exit_refused();
    if errno != 0 {
        refuse(io, company_manifest.rule_name(company_manifest.Refusal::ManifestMalformed));
    } else {
        borrow text as &tb in {
            match company_manifest.parse(heap, buffer.bytes(tb)) {
                company_manifest.ManifestResult::Refused(why) => {
                    refuse(io, company_manifest.rule_name(why));
                }
                company_manifest.ManifestResult::Held(held) => {
                    borrow held as &hm in {
                        code = with_operator(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, hm, dir, jpath, opath, sig);
                    }
                    company_manifest.drop(heap, held);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, text);
    return code;
}

// The operator file: read, parsed, checked for its shape (`company_step.operator_ok`).
fn with_operator[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &m, &d, &j, &o, &z](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, dir: &d [byte], jpath: &j [byte], opath: &o [byte], sig: &z Signals("INT,TERM")) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_read, file_write, io_write, err_write, signals("INT,TERM"), signals_read] int {
    let (text, errno) = company_cmd.read_limited(heap, fs, opath, 65536);
    var code = company_step.exit_refused();
    if errno != 0 {
        refuse(io, rule_operator_invalid());
    } else {
        borrow text as &tb in {
            let src = buffer.bytes(tb);
            let tape = box_slice(heap, json.tape_len(src), 0);
            var parsed = false;
            borrow mut tape as &!tw in {
                parsed = json.parse(src, contents(tw)) > 0;
            }
            borrow tape as &tr in {
                let tp = contents(tr);
                if parsed == false {
                    refuse(io, rule_operator_invalid());
                } else if company_step.operator_ok(src, tp) == false {
                    refuse(io, rule_operator_invalid());
                } else {
                    code = with_prices(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, hm, dir, jpath, src, tp, sig);
                }
            }
            unbox_slice(heap, tape);
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, text);
    return code;
}

// The price table the manifest names, in the company directory, and the prices of the manifest's model in it.
fn with_prices[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &m, &d, &j, &s, &t, &z](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, dir: &d [byte], jpath: &j [byte], op: &s [byte], tp: &t [int], sig: &z Signals("INT,TERM")) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_read, file_write, io_write, err_write, signals("INT,TERM"), signals_read] int {
    let ppath = company_cmd.under(heap, dir, company_manifest.price_table_of(hm));
    var code = company_step.exit_refused();
    borrow ppath as &pp in {
        let (text, errno) = company_cmd.read_limited(heap, fs, buffer.bytes(pp), 1048576);
        if errno != 0 {
            refuse(io, company_manifest.rule_name(company_manifest.Refusal::PriceTableMissing));
        } else {
            borrow text as &tb in {
                match company_budget.parse_prices_for(heap, buffer.bytes(tb), company_manifest.provider_of(hm), company_manifest.model_of(hm)) {
                    option.Option::None => {
                        refuse(io, company_manifest.rule_name(company_manifest.Refusal::PriceTableMissing));
                    }
                    option.Option::Some(prices) => {
                        code = run_loop(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, hm, dir, jpath, op, tp, prices, sig);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        buffer.drop(heap, text);
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, ppath);
    return code;
}

// Round after round until one ends the run. Each round either stops or accepts a step (or stops), so the number of rounds is at
// most the number of steps plus one; a bound twice that turns a journal write that did not take into an I/O error, never a loop.
fn run_loop[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &m, &d, &j, &s, &t, &z](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, dir: &d [byte], jpath: &j [byte], op: &s [byte], tp: &t [int], prices: company_budget.Prices, sig: &z Signals("INT,TERM")) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_read, file_write, io_write, err_write, signals("INT,TERM"), signals_read] int {
    let most = 2 * company_manifest.step_count(hm) + 2;
    var code = 0 - 1;
    var rounds = 0;
    while code < 0 {
        if rounds >= most {
            say(io, "error: the run did not settle: a step was not accepted after its record was written", "");
            code = company_step.exit_io();
        } else {
            code = one_round(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, hm, dir, jpath, op, tp, prices, sig);
            rounds = rounds + 1;
        }
    }
    return code;
}

fn limits_of[&m](hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest) -> [] company_budget.Limits {
    return company_budget.Limits { input: company_manifest.budget_input(hm), output: company_manifest.budget_output(hm), cache_write: company_manifest.budget_cache_write(hm), cache_read: company_manifest.budget_cache_read(hm) };
}

// One round: replay, ask, act. -1 to go on, else the exit status.
fn one_round[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &m, &d, &j, &s, &t, &z](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, dir: &d [byte], jpath: &j [byte], op: &s [byte], tp: &t [int], prices: company_budget.Prices, sig: &z Signals("INT,TERM")) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_read, file_write, io_write, err_write, signals("INT,TERM"), signals_read] int {
    let existed = journal_io.exists(fs, jpath);
    let (data, errno) = journal_io.read_all(heap, fs, jpath);
    if errno != 0 {
        buffer.drop(heap, data);
        say(io, "error: cannot read company.journal", "");
        return company_step.exit_io();
    }
    var action = company_run.Action::Done;
    var seq = 0;
    var before = company_budget.usage_of(0, 0, 0, 0);
    var before_cost = 0;
    var bad = 0;
    var bad_line = 0;
    var cut = 0 - 1;
    var rejected = false;
    var held_code = 0;
    var needs = buffer.empty(heap, 1);
    borrow data as &db in {
        let state = company_state.replay(heap, buffer.bytes(db), hm);
        borrow state as &sr in {
            let rep = company_state.report_of(sr);
            if rep.rule != 0 {
                bad = rep.rule;
                bad_line = rep.line;
            } else {
                cut = journal.recovery_cut(rep);
                seq = rep.records + 1;
                let spent = company_state.spent_cost_micro(sr, prices);
                action = company_run.next_action(hm, sr, limits_of(hm), spent, company_budget.dollars_to_micro(company_manifest.budget_max_cost(hm)));
                match action {
                    company_run.Action::Start(k) => {
                        before = company_state.step_recorded(sr, k);
                        before_cost = company_state.step_cost_micro(sr, k);
                        buffer.drop(heap, needs);
                        needs = company_step.needs_text(heap, hm, sr, k);
                    }
                    company_run.Action::Blocked(k) => {
                        rejected = company_state.step_blocked_by_gate(hm, sr, k) == 2;
                    }
                    company_run.Action::WaitGate(k) => {
                        rejected = false;
                    }
                    company_run.Action::Stop(tag) => {
                        rejected = false;
                    }
                    company_run.Action::Done => {
                        rejected = false;
                    }
                    company_run.Action::Held(k) => {
                        held_code = company_state.held_by_check(sr, k);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        company_state.drop(heap, state);
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, data);
    if bad != 0 {
        buffer.drop(heap, needs);
        refuse(io, task.rule_code_name(bad));
        say_nat(heap, io, "line: ", bad_line);
        return company_step.exit_refused();
    }
    if cut >= 0 {
        if journal_io.cut_torn_tail(fs, jpath, cut) != 0 {
            buffer.drop(heap, needs);
            say(io, "error: cannot cut the torn tail of company.journal", "");
            return company_step.exit_io();
        }
    }
    var started = 0 - 1;
    borrow needs as &nb in {
        match action {
            company_run.Action::Start(k) => {
                started = run_step(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, hm, dir, jpath, op, tp, prices, k, seq, existed, before, before_cost, buffer.bytes(nb), sig);
            }
            company_run.Action::WaitGate(k) => {
                started = 0 - 1;
            }
            company_run.Action::Blocked(k) => {
                started = 0 - 1;
            }
            company_run.Action::Stop(tag) => {
                started = 0 - 1;
            }
            company_run.Action::Done => {
                started = 0 - 1;
            }
            company_run.Action::Held(k) => {
                started = 0 - 1;
            }
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, needs);
    match action {
        company_run.Action::Start(k) => {
            return started;
        }
        company_run.Action::WaitGate(k) => {
            refuse(io, rule_gate_open());
            name_step(io, hm, k, true);
            return company_step.exit_wait_gate();
        }
        company_run.Action::Blocked(k) => {
            if rejected {
                refuse(io, rule_gate_rejected());
            } else {
                refuse(io, company_manifest.rule_name(company_manifest.Refusal::StepCycle));
            }
            name_step(io, hm, k, rejected);
            return company_step.exit_blocked();
        }
        company_run.Action::Stop(tag) => {
            refuse(io, company_run.stop_name(tag));
            return company_step.exit_budget();
        }
        company_run.Action::Done => {
            say_nat(heap, io, "done: steps accepted ", company_manifest.step_count(hm));
            return company_step.exit_done();
        }
        company_run.Action::Held(k) => {
            let id = company_manifest.step_id(hm, company_manifest.step_at(hm, k));
            refuse(io, company_step.held_tag(held_code));
            say(io, "step: ", id);
            say(io, "held: its last check did not pass; the run does not start it again by itself", "");
            io.write_all(io, company_step.retry_hint());
            io.write_all(io, dir);
            io.write_all(io, " retry:");
            io.write_all(io, id);
            say(io, " approved <reason>", "");
            return company_step.held_exit(held_code);
        }
    }
}

// `gate: <name>` (when `with_gate`) and `step: <id>` of step `k`.
fn name_step[&i, &m](io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, k: int, with_gate: bool) -> [io_write] int {
    let step = company_manifest.step_at(hm, k);
    if with_gate {
        say(io, "gate: ", company_manifest.step_gate(hm, step));
    }
    return say(io, "step: ", company_manifest.step_id(hm, step));
}

// ---- one step --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Append one record to the journal (a new file is made durable in its directory). 0, or the exit status of the failure (printed).
fn record[&f, &i, &j, &r](fs: &f Fs(""), io: &!i Io, jpath: &j [byte], line: &r [byte], new_file: bool) -> [fs_read(""), fs_write(""), file_write, io_write] int {
    if journal_io.append(fs, jpath, line, new_file) != 0 {
        say(io, "error: cannot write company.journal", "");
        return company_step.exit_io();
    }
    return 0;
}

fn record_buffer[&h, &f, &i, &j](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), io: &!i Io, jpath: &j [byte], ev: buffer.Buffer, new_file: bool) -> [heap, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), file_write, io_write] int {
    var status = 0;
    borrow ev as &eb in {
        status = record(fs, io, jpath, buffer.bytes(eb), new_file);
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, ev);
    return status;
}

// Is there a directory at `path`?
fn dir_present[&f, &p](fs: &f Fs(""), path: &p [byte]) -> [fs_read("")] bool {
    if journal_io.path_ok(fs, path) == false {
        return false;
    }
    match open_dir(fs, path) {
        DirOpened::Failed(e) => {
            return false;
        }
        DirOpened::Ok(d) => {
            dir_close(d);
            return true;
        }
    }
}

// Step `k`, the next record `seq`; `before` and `before_cost` are what the journal recorded for it so far.
fn run_step[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &m, &d, &j, &s, &t, &n, &z](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, dir: &d [byte], jpath: &j [byte], op: &s [byte], tp: &t [int], prices: company_budget.Prices, k: int, seq: int, existed: bool, before: company_budget.Usage, before_cost: int, needs: &n [byte], sig: &z Signals("INT,TERM")) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_read, file_write, io_write, err_write, signals("INT,TERM"), signals_read] int {
    let step = company_manifest.step_at(hm, k);
    let id = company_manifest.step_id(hm, step);
    let check = company_manifest.step_check(hm, step);
    let runner = company_step.runner_of(step.role);
    let timeout = company_step.op_ms(op, tp, "check_timeout_ms", check_timeout_default());
    match runner {
        company_step.Runner::Unsupported => {
            refuse(io, company_manifest.rule_name(company_manifest.Refusal::TaskTypeUnsupported));
            say(io, "step: ", id);
            return company_step.exit_refused();
        }
        company_step.Runner::Person => {
            let gate = company_manifest.step_gate(hm, step);
            if len(check) == 0 && len(gate) == 0 {
                refuse(io, company_manifest.rule_name(company_manifest.Refusal::CheckMissing));
                say(io, "step: ", id);
                return company_step.exit_refused();
            }
            let verified = take_needs(heap, fs, io, hm, dir, id, needs, false, dir);
            if verified != 0 {
                return verified;
            }
            let mpath = company_cmd.under(heap, dir, "company.json");
            var code = record_buffer(heap, fs, io, jpath, company_state.admitted(heap, seq, id, "person"), existed == false);
            if code == 0 {
                borrow mpath as &mp in {
                    if len(check) == 0 {
                        code = accept_unchecked(heap, fs, io, jpath, id, dir, seq + 1);
                    } else {
                        code = check_and_accept(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, dir, company_step.op_text(op, tp, "workers"), jpath, id, check, buffer.bytes(mp), "company.json", dir, "company.json", seq + 1, timeout);
                    }
                }
            }
            buffer.drop(heap, mpath);
            return code;
        }
        company_step.Runner::Build => {
            return worker_step(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, hm, dir, jpath, op, tp, prices, k, seq, existed, before, before_cost, true, needs, sig);
        }
        company_step.Runner::Gate => {
            return worker_step(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, hm, dir, jpath, op, tp, prices, k, seq, existed, before, before_cost, false, needs, sig);
        }
        company_step.Runner::Tool => {
            return tool_step(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, hm, dir, jpath, op, tp, k, seq, existed, needs);
        }
    }
}

fn tool_timeout_default() -> [] int {
    return 600000;
}

// An analyst step (task 11): `tool.run` through the tool runner (workers/tool_run.cho, `tool_call.run_step`). What runs is the
// operator's, never the manifest's: the tools directory `analyst_tools`, the list `granted_tools`, and `analysts.<id>` (the tool, its
// argv as a JSON array, its input beneath `steps/<id>/ev`, which the operator puts there). Refusals that write nothing: no check
// (`check-missing`), no execution directory (`step-dir-missing`), no `analyst_tools` or no entry for the step (`operator-invalid`),
// and every refusal of `tool_task.check` (`tool-not-granted`, `argv-malformed`, `path-invalid`, ...), exit 1, the step not admitted.
// Then the needs are verified (not delivered: the input is the operator's), the step admitted, the tool run, and its standard output
// (`tool.out`, kept by the runner) is the artifact the check judges, hashed beneath the execution directory as every artifact is. An
// analyst uses no model: no spend is read or recorded. A run that ended without a claim (a refusal of the worker, the deadline, the
// output cap, a cancel) is `step-failed`, exit 15.
fn tool_step[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &m, &d, &j, &s, &t, &n](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, dir: &d [byte], jpath: &j [byte], op: &s [byte], tp: &t [int], k: int, seq: int, existed: bool, needs: &n [byte]) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_read, file_write, io_write, err_write] int {
    let step = company_manifest.step_at(hm, k);
    let id = company_manifest.step_id(hm, step);
    let check = company_manifest.step_check(hm, step);
    if len(check) == 0 {
        refuse(io, company_manifest.rule_name(company_manifest.Refusal::CheckMissing));
        say(io, "step: ", id);
        return company_step.exit_refused();
    }
    let tools_dir = company_step.op_text(op, tp, "analyst_tools");
    let tool = company_step.op_analyst(op, tp, id, "tool");
    let argv = company_step.op_analyst_node(op, tp, id, "argv");
    let input = company_step.op_analyst(op, tp, id, "input");
    let granted = company_step.op_node(op, tp, "granted_tools");
    let timeout = company_step.op_ms(op, tp, "tool_timeout_ms", tool_timeout_default());
    let workers = company_step.op_text(op, tp, "workers");
    let rel = glue(heap, "steps/", id);
    var code = 0;
    borrow rel as &rb in {
        let stepdir = company_cmd.under(heap, dir, buffer.bytes(rb));
        borrow stepdir as &sb in {
            let evdir = glue(heap, buffer.bytes(sb), "/ev");
            borrow evdir as &eb in {
                if dir_present(fs, buffer.bytes(eb)) == false {
                    refuse(io, rule_step_dir_missing());
                    say(io, "step: ", id);
                    code = company_step.exit_refused();
                } else if len(tools_dir) == 0 || argv < 0 {
                    refuse(io, rule_operator_invalid());
                    say(io, "step: ", id);
                    code = company_step.exit_refused();
                } else {
                    match tool_task.check(tool_task.task_type(), buffer.bytes(eb), tools_dir, timeout, op, tp, granted, tool, argv, input) {
                        tool_task.Checked::Refused(why) => {
                            refuse(io, tool_task.rule_name(why));
                            say(io, "step: ", id);
                            code = company_step.exit_refused();
                        }
                        tool_task.Checked::Held => {
                            code = take_needs(heap, fs, io, hm, dir, id, needs, false, buffer.bytes(eb));
                            if code == 0 {
                                code = record_buffer(heap, fs, io, jpath, company_state.admitted(heap, seq, id, buffer.bytes(rb)), existed == false);
                            }
                            if code == 0 {
                                let ran = tool_call.run_step(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, timeout, buffer.bytes(sb), workers, tools_dir, op, tp, granted, tool, argv, input, buffer.bytes(eb));
                                if ran != 0 {
                                    refuse(io, rule_step_failed());
                                    say(io, "step: ", id);
                                    say_nat(heap, io, "exit: ", ran);
                                    code = company_step.exit_step_failed();
                                } else {
                                    let name = company_step.artifact_name(company_step.Runner::Tool);
                                    let artifact = company_cmd.under(heap, buffer.bytes(eb), name);
                                    let shown = buffer.append(heap, glue(heap, buffer.bytes(rb), "/ev/"), name);
                                    borrow artifact as &ab in {
                                        borrow shown as &wb in {
                                            code = check_and_accept(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, dir, workers, jpath, id, check, buffer.bytes(ab), buffer.bytes(wb), buffer.bytes(eb), name, seq + 1, company_step.op_ms(op, tp, "check_timeout_ms", check_timeout_default()));
                                        }
                                    }
                                    buffer.drop(heap, artifact);
                                    buffer.drop(heap, shown);
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            buffer.drop(heap, evdir);
        }
        buffer.drop(heap, stepdir);
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, rel);
    return code;
}

// A product step with no check and an approved gate: accepted on the decision, its artifact the manifest Forge hashes now.
fn accept_unchecked[&h, &f, &i, &j, &n, &d](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), io: &!i Io, jpath: &j [byte], id: &n [byte], dir: &d [byte], seq: int) -> [heap, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, file_read, file_write, io_write] int {
    let (sha, why) = hash_artifact(heap, fs, dir, "company.json");
    var code = 0;
    borrow sha as &hb in {
        if why != 0 {
            code = refuse_artifact(heap, fs, io, jpath, id, "company.json", why, seq);
        } else {
            code = record_buffer(heap, fs, io, jpath, company_state.accepted(heap, seq, id, "company.json", buffer.bytes(hb)), false);
            if code == 0 {
                say(io, "accepted: ", id);
                code = 0 - 1;
            }
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, sha);
    return code;
}

// A builder (`build`) or qa step: refusals, admission, the worker, the spend, the check.
fn worker_step[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &m, &d, &j, &s, &t, &n, &z](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, dir: &d [byte], jpath: &j [byte], op: &s [byte], tp: &t [int], prices: company_budget.Prices, k: int, seq: int, existed: bool, before: company_budget.Usage, before_cost: int, build: bool, needs: &n [byte], sig: &z Signals("INT,TERM")) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_read, file_write, io_write, err_write, signals("INT,TERM"), signals_read] int {
    let step = company_manifest.step_at(hm, k);
    let id = company_manifest.step_id(hm, step);
    let check = company_manifest.step_check(hm, step);
    if len(check) == 0 {
        refuse(io, company_manifest.rule_name(company_manifest.Refusal::CheckMissing));
        say(io, "step: ", id);
        return company_step.exit_refused();
    }
    var ready = true;
    if build {
        if len(company_step.op_text(op, tp, "tools")) == 0 || len(company_step.op_text(op, tp, "checker")) == 0 || len(company_step.op_text(op, tp, "agent")) == 0 {
            ready = false;
        }
    } else if len(company_step.op_gate(op, tp, id, "repo")) == 0 || len(company_step.op_gate(op, tp, id, "revision")) == 0 || len(company_step.op_gate(op, tp, id, "checkout")) == 0 {
        ready = false;
    }
    let rel = glue(heap, "steps/", id);
    var stepdir = buffer.empty(heap, 1);
    borrow rel as &rb in {
        buffer.drop(heap, stepdir);
        stepdir = company_cmd.under(heap, dir, buffer.bytes(rb));
    }
    var code = 0;
    borrow rel as &rb in {
        borrow stepdir as &sb in {
            let evdir = glue(heap, buffer.bytes(sb), "/ev");
            borrow evdir as &eb in {
                if dir_present(fs, buffer.bytes(eb)) == false {
                    refuse(io, rule_step_dir_missing());
                    say(io, "step: ", id);
                    code = company_step.exit_refused();
                } else if ready == false {
                    refuse(io, rule_operator_invalid());
                    say(io, "step: ", id);
                    code = company_step.exit_refused();
                } else {
                    if build {
                        code = reap_stale(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, company_step.op_text(op, tp, "workers"), id, buffer.bytes(sb), buffer.bytes(eb));
                    }
                    if code == 0 {
                        code = take_needs(heap, fs, io, hm, dir, id, needs, build, buffer.bytes(eb));
                    }
                    if code == 0 && build {
                        code = take_contract(heap, fs, io, hm, dir, step, buffer.bytes(eb));
                    }
                    if code == 0 {
                        code = record_buffer(heap, fs, io, jpath, company_state.admitted(heap, seq, id, buffer.bytes(rb)), existed == false);
                    }
                    if code == 0 {
                        code = worker_run(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, hm, dir, jpath, op, tp, prices, k, seq + 1, before, before_cost, build, buffer.bytes(rb), buffer.bytes(sb), buffer.bytes(eb), sig);
                    }
                }
            }
            buffer.drop(heap, evdir);
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, rel);
    buffer.drop(heap, stepdir);
    return code;
}

// A build of this step that an earlier run started and never saw end (task 9): that run was killed, and the Cancho Code adapter's
// supervisor and the build it started, which run in sessions of their own so that a build outlives a Forge that is gone
// (docs/async-level.md), may still be running in `evdir`. Before the step is admitted again, every task of the step's workspace
// whose worker may be running (`recover.in_flight`: phase 2 or 5) is asked to stop through the adapter (`await_run.cancel`, the
// verb `forge cancel` uses: the supervisor kills the build's group and keeps the result), a task in phase 5 is then settled as
// `forge recover <dir> <workers>` settles it (`await_task.run`, without waiting: the run is cancelled), and the rest of the
// journal is recovered (`recover_io.run`: phase 2 becomes `execution.lost`, then `ready`). `reaped: <task>` is printed for each
// task asked. 0 to go on; `exit_step_failed` when a run of an earlier Forge is still going after it was asked to stop. The
// workspace lock (`<stepdir>/lock`) is taken for it; a lock someone holds, or a workspace with no journal, is left to the build.
fn reap_stale[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &w, &s, &n, &e](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, workers: &w [byte], step: &s [byte], stepdir: &n [byte], evdir: &e [byte]) -> [heap, exec_under, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, file_read, file_write, io_write] int {
    let jpath = glue(heap, stepdir, "/journal.jsonl");
    let lpath = glue(heap, stepdir, "/lock");
    var code = 0;
    borrow jpath as &jb in {
        borrow lpath as &lb in {
            if journal_io.path_ok(fs, workers) && journal_io.exists(fs, buffer.bytes(jb)) {
                match lock.acquire(fs, buffer.bytes(lb)) {
                    lock.Lock::Refused(rule) => {
                        code = 0;
                    }
                    lock.Lock::Failed(e) => {
                        code = 0;
                    }
                    lock.Lock::Held(held) => {
                        code = reap_locked(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, workers, step, buffer.bytes(jb), evdir);
                        let recover_io.Settled { rule, errno, text, counts } = recover_io.run(heap, fs, buffer.bytes(jb));
                        buffer.drop(heap, text);
                        lock.let_go(held);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, jpath);
    buffer.drop(heap, lpath);
    return code;
}

fn reap_locked[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &w, &s, &j, &e](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, workers: &w [byte], step: &s [byte], jpath: &j [byte], evdir: &e [byte]) -> [heap, exec_under, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, file_read, file_write, io_write] int {
    let (data, errno) = journal_io.read_all(heap, fs, jpath);
    if errno != 0 {
        buffer.drop(heap, data);
        return 0;
    }
    var code = 0;
    match open_dir(fs, workers) {
        DirOpened::Failed(e) => {
            code = 0;
        }
        DirOpened::Ok(opened) => {
            var wdir = opened;
            borrow wdir as &wd in {
                borrow data as &db in {
                    let ids = recover.in_flight(heap, buffer.bytes(db));
                    borrow ids as &ib in {
                        let all = buffer.bytes(ib);
                        var start = 0;
                        var k = 0;
                        while k < len(all) {
                            if int_of(all[k]) == 10 {
                                let one = reap_one(heap, fs, exec, wd, clock, io, buffer.bytes(db), all[start..k], step, jpath, evdir);
                                if one != 0 && code == 0 {
                                    code = one;
                                }
                                start = k + 1;
                            }
                            k = k + 1;
                        }
                    }
                    buffer.drop(heap, ids);
                }
            }
            dir_close(wdir);
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, data);
    return code;
}

fn reap_one[&h, &f, &x, &w, &c, &i, &d, &t, &s, &j, &e](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), workers: &w Dir, clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, data: &d [byte], tid: &t [byte], step: &s [byte], jpath: &j [byte], evdir: &e [byte]) -> [heap, exec_under, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, file_read, file_write, io_write] int {
    let (phase, request, xdir) = recover.accepted_of(heap, data, tid);
    buffer.drop(heap, xdir);
    var req = request;
    if phase != 5 {
        buffer.drop(heap, req);
        req = gate_run.request_id_of(heap, tid, 1);
    }
    say(io, "reaped: ", tid);
    borrow req as &rb in {
        await_run.cancel(heap, exec, workers, clock, "cancho_code", buffer.bytes(rb), tid, evdir);
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, req);
    if phase != 5 {
        return 0;
    }
    let done = await_task.run(heap, fs, exec, workers, clock, jpath, tid, 1000, false);
    match done.outcome {
        await_task.Outcome::Running => {
            refuse(io, rule_step_failed());
            say(io, "step: ", step);
            say(io, "reason: a build an earlier run started is still running after it was asked to stop: ", tid);
            return company_step.exit_step_failed();
        }
        await_task.Outcome::Journal(rule) => {
            return 0;
        }
        await_task.Outcome::NotAwaiting => {
            return 0;
        }
        await_task.Outcome::Ended(ending, claim) => {
            return 0;
        }
    }
}

// The step is admitted: run its worker, record a build's spend, then the check (`seq` is the next record).
fn worker_run[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &m, &d, &j, &s, &t, &r, &w, &e, &z](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, dir: &d [byte], jpath: &j [byte], op: &s [byte], tp: &t [int], prices: company_budget.Prices, k: int, seq: int, before: company_budget.Usage, before_cost: int, build: bool, rel: &r [byte], stepdir: &w [byte], evdir: &e [byte], sig: &z Signals("INT,TERM")) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, file_read, file_write, io_write, err_write, signals("INT,TERM"), signals_read] int {
    let step = company_manifest.step_at(hm, k);
    let id = company_manifest.step_id(hm, step);
    let workers = company_step.op_text(op, tp, "workers");
    var next = seq;
    var ran = 0;
    if build {
        let wrote = write_brief(heap, fs, hm, step, stepdir);
        if wrote != 0 {
            say(io, "error: cannot write the brief of step ", id);
            return company_step.exit_io();
        }
        let brief = glue(heap, stepdir, "/brief.txt");
        var contract = "";
        if len(company_manifest.step_contract(hm, step)) > 0 {
            contract = company_files.contract_name();
        }
        var spec = step_run.build_spec();
        let bt = company_step.op_ms(op, tp, "build_timeout_ms", spec.timeout);
        spec = step_run.BuildSpec { timeout: bt, attempts: spec.attempts, agent_seconds: spec.agent_seconds, rounds: spec.rounds, plan_seconds: spec.plan_seconds, jobs: spec.jobs };
        // TERM and INT are claimed for the build only (task 9): a signal then cancels the build through its worker (the build's
        // group is killed) before Forge exits. Outside the build they are not claimed, so a signal ends Forge the default way;
        // a check's guard then sees Forge gone and kills the check (workers/check_guard.cho).
        var stopped = false;
        var stop: option.Option[SignalWatch] = option.Option::None;
        match signals_watch(sig) {
            Watching::Ok(wt) => {
                // `stop` is None here; it is matched so that the linear value is consumed before it is replaced
                match stop {
                    option.Option::Some(none) => {
                        signals_close(none);
                    }
                    option.Option::None => {
                    }
                }
                stop = option.Option::Some(wt);
            }
            Watching::Failed(e) => {
                // the runtime refused the claim (another claim, a live thread: docs/signals.md): the build runs as `forge build`
                // does, and a signal ends Forge the default way
                stopped = false;
            }
        }
        borrow brief as &bb in {
            borrow mut stop as &!sp in {
                let (r, asked) = step_run.run_build(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, spec, stepdir, workers, company_step.op_text(op, tp, "tools"), buffer.bytes(bb), evdir, company_step.op_text(op, tp, "checker"), company_manifest.provider_of(hm), company_manifest.model_of(hm), company_step.op_text(op, tp, "agent"), "", contract, sp);
                ran = r;
                stopped = asked;
            }
        }
        buffer.drop(heap, brief);
        // What the build spent is recorded before the claim ends: a second signal then ends Forge at once.
        let (spent_code, after_seq) = record_spend(heap, fs, io, jpath, id, evdir, prices, before, before_cost, next);
        // A signal that came after the build's last look is read here rather than let through by the close (it would end Forge
        // the default way): the run then stops as for one during the build (fix round 1).
        borrow mut stop as &!sp in {
            if await_run.stop_asked(sp) {
                stopped = true;
            }
        }
        match stop {
            option.Option::Some(w2) => {
                signals_close(w2);
            }
            option.Option::None => {
            }
        }
        if spent_code != 0 {
            return spent_code;
        }
        next = after_seq;
        if stopped {
            say(io, "stopped: signal", "");
            say(io, "step: ", id);
            return company_step.exit_stopped();
        }
    } else {
        let gt = company_step.op_ms(op, tp, "gate_timeout_ms", step_run.gate_spec().timeout);
        ran = step_run.run_gate(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, step_run.GateSpec { timeout: gt }, stepdir, workers, company_step.op_gate(op, tp, id, "repo"), company_step.op_gate(op, tp, id, "revision"), company_step.op_gate(op, tp, id, "checkout"), evdir, "");
    }
    if ran != 0 {
        refuse(io, rule_step_failed());
        say(io, "step: ", id);
        say_nat(heap, io, "exit: ", ran);
        return company_step.exit_step_failed();
    }
    // A build that changed its contract (the copy Forge wrote in its execution directory) is not accepted: the spend is recorded
    // above, the step is held as an unavailable check (`artifact-invalid`, `reason: contract-changed`), the check is not run.
    if build && len(company_manifest.step_contract(hm, step)) > 0 {
        if !company_files.contract_intact(heap, fs, dir, company_manifest.step_contract(hm, step), evdir) {
            let shown_contract = glue(heap, rel, "/ev/contract.txt");
            var refused = 0;
            borrow shown_contract as &scb in {
                refused = refuse_artifact(heap, fs, io, jpath, id, buffer.bytes(scb), 6, next);
            }
            buffer.drop(heap, shown_contract);
            return refused;
        }
    }
    var name = company_step.artifact_name(company_step.Runner::Gate);
    if build {
        name = company_step.artifact_name(company_step.Runner::Build);
    }
    let artifact = company_cmd.under(heap, evdir, name);
    var shown = buffer.append(heap, glue(heap, rel, "/ev/"), name);
    var code = 0;
    borrow artifact as &ab in {
        borrow shown as &wb in {
            code = check_and_accept(heap, fs, exec, clock, io, dir, workers, jpath, id, company_manifest.step_check(hm, step), buffer.bytes(ab), buffer.bytes(wb), evdir, name, next, company_step.op_ms(op, tp, "check_timeout_ms", check_timeout_default()));
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, artifact);
    buffer.drop(heap, shown);
    return code;
}

// The step's contract, when it names one: the file of the company directory is read and written as `contract.txt` in the execution
// directory `evdir` (`company_files.deliver_contract`). Not there, not a regular file, too large or unreadable: `refusal:
// artifact-invalid`, `step:`, `contract:`, `reason: contract-missing|contract-unreadable`, exit 14, and nothing admitted or recorded
// (the step is not at fault; the next run reads the file again). The execution directory cannot be written: an I/O error. 0 or the
// exit status.
fn take_contract[&h, &f, &i, &m, &d, &e](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, dir: &d [byte], step: company_manifest.Step, evdir: &e [byte]) -> [heap, fs_read(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_read, file_write, io_write] int {
    let name = company_manifest.step_contract(hm, step);
    if len(name) == 0 {
        company_files.clear_contract(fs, evdir);
        return 0;
    }
    let id = company_manifest.step_id(hm, step);
    let why = company_files.deliver_contract(heap, fs, dir, name, evdir);
    if why == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if why == 6 {
        say(io, "error: cannot give step ", id);
        return company_step.exit_io();
    }
    refuse(io, company_step.rule_artifact_invalid());
    say(io, "step: ", id);
    say(io, "contract: ", name);
    say(io, "reason: ", company_step.contract_reason(why));
    return company_step.exit_check_unavailable();
}

// The step's `input`, decoded from its JSON string, written to `<stepdir>/brief.txt` (truncated first). 0 or an errno (-2: a path
// that is not ok).
fn write_brief[&h, &f, &m, &w](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, step: company_manifest.Step, stepdir: &w [byte]) -> [heap, fs_write(""), file_write] int {
    let text = company_manifest.step_input(hm, step);
    let quoted = glue(heap, "\"", text);
    var q = buffer.append(heap, quoted, "\"");
    var rc = 0 - 2;
    borrow q as &qb in {
        let src = buffer.bytes(qb);
        let tape = box_slice(heap, json.tape_len(src), 0);
        borrow mut tape as &!tw in {
            let tp = contents(tw);
            if json.parse(src, tp) > 0 {
                if json.is_string(tp, 0) {
                    let n = json.string_length(src, tp, 0);
                    if n >= 0 {
                        let out = box_slice(heap, n, byte_of(0));
                        borrow mut out as &!ow in {
                            json.string_into(src, tp, 0, contents(ow));
                        }
                        let path = glue(heap, stepdir, "/brief.txt");
                        borrow path as &pb in {
                            borrow out as &or in {
                                rc = write_file(fs, buffer.bytes(pb), contents(or));
                            }
                        }
                        buffer.drop(heap, path);
                        unbox_slice(heap, out);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        unbox_slice(heap, tape);
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, q);
    return rc;
}

fn write_file[&f, &p, &b](fs: &f Fs(""), path: &p [byte], contents: &b [byte]) -> [fs_write(""), file_write] int {
    if journal_io.path_ok(fs, path) == false {
        return 0 - 2;
    }
    match open_write(fs, path) {
        Opened::Failed(e) => {
            return e;
        }
        Opened::Ok(opened) => {
            var file = opened;
            var rc = 0;
            borrow mut file as &!x in {
                rc = journal_io.write_all(x, contents);
            }
            file_close(file);
            return rc;
        }
    }
}

// The build's `usage.jsonl` (none is no usage), priced call by call, less what was recorded for the step: appended as one priced
// spend record when anything is new. (0 or the exit status of a failure, the next record).
fn record_spend[&h, &f, &i, &j, &n, &e](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), io: &!i Io, jpath: &j [byte], id: &n [byte], evdir: &e [byte], prices: company_budget.Prices, before: company_budget.Usage, before_cost: int, seq: int) -> [heap, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, file_read, file_write, io_write] (int, int) {
    var code = 0;
    var next = seq;
    // Read like an artifact (final review, I1): beneath the execution directory, no link followed, a regular file of at most 16 MiB,
    // a FIFO never opened. Not there is no usage (1); anything else is `usage-invalid`, recorded, and the step held.
    let (text, why) = company_files.read_artifact(heap, fs, evdir, "usage.jsonl");
    if why != 0 && why != 1 {
        code = record_buffer(heap, fs, io, jpath, company_state.check_unavailable(heap, seq, id, company_step.rule_usage_invalid()), false);
        if code == 0 {
            refuse(io, company_step.rule_usage_invalid());
            say(io, "step: ", id);
            say(io, "reason: the build's usage.jsonl is ", company_step.artifact_reason(why));
            code = company_step.exit_check_unavailable();
            next = seq + 1;
        }
    } else {
        borrow text as &tb in {
            let after = company_budget.parse_usage_for(buffer.bytes(tb), prices);
            let delta = company_step.usage_delta(after, before);
            let cost = company_step.cost_delta(company_budget.cost_micro(after, prices), before_cost);
            if company_step.any_tokens(delta) || cost > 0 {
                code = record_buffer(heap, fs, io, jpath, company_state.spend_priced(heap, seq, id, delta.input, delta.output, delta.cache_write, delta.cache_read, cost), false);
                next = seq + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, text);
    return (code, next);
}

// ---- the check -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The SHA-256 of `data` as lower-case hex.
fn hex_of[&h, &b](heap: &!h Heap, data: &b [byte]) -> [heap] buffer.Buffer {
    let digest = box_slice(heap, 32, byte_of(0));
    borrow mut digest as &!dw in {
        crypto.sha256(data, contents(dw));
    }
    var hex = buffer.empty(heap, 64);
    borrow digest as &dg in {
        hex = company_step.put_hex(heap, hex, contents(dg));
    }
    unbox_slice(heap, digest);
    return hex;
}

// The SHA-256 of the artifact `name` beneath the directory `dir` as hex, and 0, or why it is refused (`read_artifact`).
fn hash_artifact[&h, &f, &d, &n](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), dir: &d [byte], name: &n [byte]) -> [heap, fs_read(""), dir_read, file_read] (buffer.Buffer, int) {
    let (data, why) = company_files.read_artifact(heap, fs, dir, name);
    var hex = buffer.empty(heap, 64);
    if why == 0 {
        buffer.drop(heap, hex);
        borrow data as &db in {
            hex = hex_of(heap, buffer.bytes(db));
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, data);
    return (hex, why);
}

// ---- needs (task 8c) -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The needs of step `id`, `needs` (`company_step.needs_text`, from the journal). FIRST every need is checked (`check_need`) and its
// bytes kept: each artifact is read again beneath its directory by `read_artifact`, as at acceptance, and must be the step's own
// artifact (`company_step.artifact_rel`) with the SHA-256 the journal recorded. The first that is not stops the step before it is
// admitted: `refusal: artifact-invalid`, `step:`, `need:`, `artifact:`, `reason: needs-missing|needs-changed`, exit 14, and
// NOTHING is written or removed anywhere (not even a hold: the step is not at fault, and the next run checks again, so putting the
// accepted bytes back lets it run). Only when all passed, and with `deliver` (a builder or writer step): the names Forge owns in
// `evdir` (`needs.json` and every `needs-*`) left by an earlier start are removed, then the very bytes checked are written,
// `needs-<need id>-<artifact name>` each, and `needs.json` last (none for a step with nothing to give). 0 or the exit status.
fn take_needs[&h, &f, &i, &m, &d, &n, &k, &e](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, dir: &d [byte], id: &n [byte], needs: &k [byte], deliver: bool, evdir: &e [byte]) -> [heap, fs_read(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_read, file_write, io_write] int {
    if len(needs) == 0 && deliver == false {
        return 0;
    }
    var code = 0;
    var count = 0;
    let tape = box_slice(heap, json.tape_len(needs), 0);
    borrow mut tape as &!tw in {
        if len(needs) > 0 {
            if json.parse(needs, contents(tw)) <= 0 {
                code = company_step.exit_io();
            } else if json.is_array(contents(tw), 0) == false {
                code = company_step.exit_io();
            } else {
                count = json.count(contents(tw), 0);
            }
        }
    }
    if code != 0 {
        say(io, "error: cannot read the needs of step ", id);
    }
    // Every need checked, its bytes appended to `all`, and where in `spans` (start, length).
    var all = buffer.empty(heap, 256);
    let spans = box_slice(heap, 2 * count + 2, 0);
    borrow tape as &tr in {
        let tp = contents(tr);
        var j = 0;
        while j < count && code == 0 {
            let (data, c) = check_need(heap, fs, io, hm, dir, id, needs, tp, json.at(tp, 0, j));
            code = c;
            var start = 0;
            borrow all as &ab in {
                start = buffer.size(ab);
            }
            borrow data as &db in {
                all = buffer.append(heap, all, buffer.bytes(db));
                borrow mut spans as &!sw in {
                    contents(sw)[2 * j] = start;
                    contents(sw)[2 * j + 1] = buffer.size(db);
                }
            }
            buffer.drop(heap, data);
            j = j + 1;
        }
        if code == 0 && deliver {
            borrow all as &ab in {
                borrow spans as &sb in {
                    code = give_needs(heap, fs, io, hm, id, needs, tp, count, buffer.bytes(ab), contents(sb), evdir);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, all);
    unbox_slice(heap, spans);
    unbox_slice(heap, tape);
    return code;
}

// All needs passed: clear what an earlier start left in `evdir`, write each copy (`all` sliced by `spans`), then `needs.json`.
fn give_needs[&h, &f, &i, &m, &n, &k, &t, &a, &s, &e](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, id: &n [byte], needs: &k [byte], tp: &t [int], count: int, all: &a [byte], sp: &s [int], evdir: &e [byte]) -> [heap, fs_read(""), dir_read, dir_write, file_write, io_write] int {
    var code = 0;
    if journal_io.path_ok(fs, evdir) == false {
        say(io, "error: cannot open the execution directory of step ", id);
        return company_step.exit_io();
    }
    match open_dir(fs, evdir) {
        DirOpened::Failed(e) => {
            say(io, "error: cannot open the execution directory of step ", id);
            code = company_step.exit_io();
        }
        DirOpened::Ok(opened) => {
            var ed = opened;
            borrow ed as &edr in {
                clear_needs(heap, edr);
                var j = 0;
                while j < count && code == 0 {
                    let need = json.string_view(needs, tp, json.get(needs, tp, json.at(tp, 0, j), "step"));
                    let at = company_graph.index_of(hm, need);
                    var runner = company_step.Runner::Unsupported;
                    if at >= 0 {
                        runner = company_step.runner_of(company_manifest.step_at(hm, at).role);
                    }
                    let name = company_step.needs_name(heap, need, runner);
                    borrow name as &nb in {
                        if company_files.put_beneath(edr, buffer.bytes(nb), all[sp[2 * j]..sp[2 * j] + sp[2 * j + 1]]) != 0 {
                            say(io, "error: cannot give step ", id);
                            code = company_step.exit_io();
                        }
                    }
                    buffer.drop(heap, name);
                    j = j + 1;
                }
                if code == 0 && len(needs) > 0 {
                    if company_files.put_beneath(edr, company_step.needs_file(), needs) != 0 {
                        say(io, "error: cannot write needs.json for step ", id);
                        code = company_step.exit_io();
                    }
                }
            }
            dir_close(ed);
        }
    }
    return code;
}

// Remove beneath `d` every name Forge gives a step (`needs.json`, `needs-*`), left by an earlier start: what a step is given is
// exactly the current needs. `unlinkat` removes a link itself; a directory of such a name is left (its removal fails, and the
// write over it then fails as an I/O error).
fn clear_needs[&h, &d](heap: &!h Heap, d: &d Dir) -> [heap, dir_read, dir_write] int {
    let names = dirs.list(heap, d, 65536);
    var removed = 0;
    borrow names as &nr in {
        var i = 0;
        while i < dirs.count(nr) {
            let nm = dirs.name(nr, i);
            var ours = bytes.equal(nm, company_step.needs_file());
            if len(nm) > 6 {
                if bytes.equal(nm[0..6], "needs-") {
                    ours = true;
                }
            }
            if ours {
                match dir_remove(d, nm) {
                    Done::Ok(n) => {
                        removed = removed + 1;
                    }
                    Done::Failed(e) => {
                        removed = removed;
                    }
                }
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }
    dirs.drop(heap, names);
    return removed;
}

// One need, the object at `node` of the needs list `src`, checked: its bytes and 0, or the exit status of the refusal (printed).
fn check_need[&h, &f, &i, &m, &d, &n, &s, &t](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), io: &!i Io, hm: &m company_manifest.Manifest, dir: &d [byte], id: &n [byte], src: &s [byte], tp: &t [int], node: int) -> [heap, fs_read(""), dir_read, file_read, io_write] (buffer.Buffer, int) {
    let need = json.string_view(src, tp, json.get(src, tp, node, "step"));
    let art = json.get(src, tp, node, "artifact");
    let sha = json.get(src, tp, node, "sha256");
    let at = company_graph.index_of(hm, need);
    var runner = company_step.Runner::Unsupported;
    if at >= 0 {
        runner = company_step.runner_of(company_manifest.step_at(hm, at).role);
    }
    let rel = company_step.artifact_rel(heap, need, runner);
    var why = 2;
    var data = buffer.empty(heap, 1);
    borrow rel as &rb in {
        if buffer.size(rb) > 0 && json.string_equals(src, tp, art, buffer.bytes(rb)) {
            // The directory the artifact is beneath: the company directory for a person's step (`company.json`, which
            // `needs_text` leaves out), else the need's execution directory (`artifact_rel` matched, so the runner is real).
            var adir = buffer.append(heap, buffer.empty(heap, 64), dir);
            if bytes.equal(buffer.bytes(rb), company_step.artifact_name(company_step.Runner::Person)) == false {
                adir = buffer.append(heap, buffer.append(heap, buffer.append(heap, adir, "/steps/"), need), "/ev");
            }
            borrow adir as &ab in {
                buffer.drop(heap, data);
                let (got, w) = company_files.read_artifact(heap, fs, buffer.bytes(ab), company_step.artifact_name(runner));
                data = got;
                why = w;
            }
            buffer.drop(heap, adir);
            if why == 0 {
                borrow data as &db in {
                    let hex = hex_of(heap, buffer.bytes(db));
                    borrow hex as &hb in {
                        if json.string_equals(src, tp, sha, buffer.bytes(hb)) == false {
                            why = 5;
                        }
                    }
                    buffer.drop(heap, hex);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, rel);
    if why != 0 {
        refuse(io, company_step.rule_artifact_invalid());
        say(io, "step: ", id);
        say(io, "need: ", need);
        say(io, "artifact: ", json.string_view(src, tp, art));
        say(io, "reason: ", company_step.needs_reason(why));
        return (data, company_step.exit_check_unavailable());
    }
    return (data, 0);
}

// The artifact of step `id` is refused (`why`, `company_step.artifact_reason`): print it and record it as an unavailable check,
// which holds the step. The exit status.
fn refuse_artifact[&h, &f, &i, &j, &n, &w](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), io: &!i Io, jpath: &j [byte], id: &n [byte], shown: &w [byte], why: int, seq: int) -> [heap, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), file_write, io_write] int {
    let wrote = record_buffer(heap, fs, io, jpath, company_state.check_unavailable(heap, seq, id, company_step.rule_artifact_invalid()), false);
    if wrote != 0 {
        return wrote;
    }
    refuse(io, company_step.rule_artifact_invalid());
    say(io, "step: ", id);
    say(io, "artifact: ", shown);
    say(io, "reason: ", company_step.artifact_reason(why));
    return company_step.exit_check_unavailable();
}

// Run the check `check` (a name in the company directory `dir`) with the arguments <artifact> <argdir>, PATH its environment, no
// input, at most `company_step.check_output_cap` bytes of output and `timeout` ms, through its guard (`company_step.guard_name`,
// beneath the operator's `workers` directory: workers/check_guard.cho). Forge holds the lock `<dir>/check.lock` while it waits, so
// the guard kills the check's group if Forge is gone; Forge's own deadline for the guard is the check's plus
// `company_step.guard_margin_ms`. How the check ended (`company_step.check_ran` of the guard's ending) and what it wrote.
fn run_check[&h, &f, &x, &c, &d, &o, &n, &a, &g](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, dir: &d [byte], workers: &o [byte], check: &n [byte], artifact: &a [byte], argdir: &g [byte], timeout: int) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), file_write] (buffer.Buffer, process.Ran) {
    if worker.name_ok(exec, check) == false || worker.name_ok(exec, company_step.guard_name()) == false || journal_io.path_ok(fs, workers) == false {
        return (buffer.empty(heap, 1), process.Ran::Failed(0 - 2));
    }
    let gpath = company_cmd.under(heap, workers, company_step.guard_name());
    var present = false;
    borrow gpath as &gp in {
        present = journal_io.exists(fs, buffer.bytes(gp));
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, gpath);
    if present == false {
        return (buffer.empty(heap, 1), process.Ran::Failed(company_step.guard_missing()));
    }
    var limit = timeout;
    if limit > 999999999 {
        limit = 999999999;
    }
    if limit < 1 {
        limit = 1;
    }
    let live = company_cmd.under(heap, dir, company_step.guard_lock());
    var out = buffer.empty(heap, 1);
    var ran = process.Ran::Failed(0 - 2);
    borrow live as &lv in {
        // The guard of a check of an earlier run lets go of this lock the moment it sees that run gone; wait for it a little.
        var tries = 0;
        var waiting = true;
        while waiting {
            match lock.acquire(fs, buffer.bytes(lv)) {
                lock.Lock::Held(held) => {
                    waiting = false;
                    buffer.drop(heap, out);
                    let (o, r) = guarded(heap, fs, exec, clock, dir, workers, check, artifact, argdir, limit, buffer.bytes(lv));
                    out = o;
                    ran = r;
                    lock.let_go(held);
                }
                lock.Lock::Refused(rule) => {
                    tries = tries + 1;
                    if tries >= 50 {
                        waiting = false;
                        ran = process.Ran::Failed(company_step.guard_busy());
                    } else {
                        await_run.nap(heap, 100);
                    }
                }
                lock.Lock::Failed(e) => {
                    waiting = false;
                    ran = process.Ran::Failed(e);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, live);
    return (out, ran);
}

// The guard started and waited for; the lock `live` is held by the caller.
fn guarded[&h, &f, &x, &c, &d, &o, &n, &a, &g, &l](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, dir: &d [byte], workers: &o [byte], check: &n [byte], artifact: &a [byte], argdir: &g [byte], limit: int, live: &l [byte]) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read("")] (buffer.Buffer, process.Ran) {
    match open_dir(fs, workers) {
        DirOpened::Failed(e) => {
            return (buffer.empty(heap, 1), process.Ran::Failed(e));
        }
        DirOpened::Ok(opened) => {
            var wdir = opened;
            var list = process.argv(heap, 512);
            let ms = buffer.push_nat(heap, buffer.empty(heap, 12), limit);
            var failed = 0;
            borrow ms as &mb in {
                let (l1, e1) = process.add(heap, list, dir);
                let (l2, e2) = process.add(heap, l1, check);
                let (l3, e3) = process.add(heap, l2, artifact);
                let (l4, e4) = process.add(heap, l3, argdir);
                let (l5, e5) = process.add(heap, l4, buffer.bytes(mb));
                let (l6, e6) = process.add(heap, l5, live);
                list = l6;
                failed = e1 + e2 + e3 + e4 + e5 + e6;
            }
            buffer.drop(heap, ms);
            var env = buffer.append(heap, buffer.empty(heap, 64), "PATH=");
            env = buffer.append(heap, env, gate_env.fixed_path());
            env = buffer.push(heap, env, byte_of(0));
            var out = buffer.empty(heap, 1);
            var ran = process.Ran::Failed(0 - 2);
            if failed == 0 {
                match process.channels() {
                    process.Channels::Failed(e) => {
                        ran = process.Ran::Failed(e);
                    }
                    process.Channels::Ok(to_child, child_in, from_child, child_out) => {
                        borrow wdir as &dw in {
                            borrow list as &lb in {
                                borrow env as &vb in {
                                    match exec_spawn_detached_under(exec, dw, company_step.guard_name(), process.list(lb), buffer.bytes(vb), Stdio::Pipe(child_in), Stdio::Pipe(child_out), Stdio::Null) {
                                        Spawned::Failed(e) => {
                                            process.close(to_child, from_child);
                                            ran = process.Ran::Failed(e);
                                        }
                                        Spawned::Ok(child) => {
                                            buffer.drop(heap, out);
                                            let (o, r) = process.capture(heap, clock, child, to_child, from_child, "", company_step.check_output_cap(), limit + company_step.guard_margin_ms());
                                            out = o;
                                            ran = company_step.check_ran(r);
                                        }
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            process.drop(heap, list);
            buffer.drop(heap, env);
            dir_close(wdir);
            return (out, ran);
        }
    }
}

// Hash the artifact, run the check, hash it again, and write what the verdict says (the head of this file, step 6). -1 to go on
// (accepted), else the exit status.
fn check_and_accept[&h, &f, &x, &c, &i, &d, &o, &j, &n, &k, &a, &w, &g](heap: &!h Heap, fs: &f Fs(""), exec: &x Exec(""), clock: &c Clock, io: &!i Io, dir: &d [byte], workers: &o [byte], jpath: &j [byte], id: &n [byte], check: &k [byte], artifact: &a [byte], shown: &w [byte], argdir: &g [byte], name: &static [byte], seq: int, timeout: int) -> [heap, exec_under, exec_detached, clock, poll, fs_read(""), fs_write(""), dir_read, file_read, file_write, io_write] int {
    let (first, why) = hash_artifact(heap, fs, argdir, name);
    if why != 0 {
        buffer.drop(heap, first);
        return refuse_artifact(heap, fs, io, jpath, id, shown, why, seq);
    }
    let (out, ran) = run_check(heap, fs, exec, clock, dir, workers, check, artifact, argdir, timeout);
    var verdict = 2;
    var score = 0 - 1;
    var total = 0 - 1;
    borrow out as &ob in {
        verdict = company_step.check_verdict(ran, buffer.bytes(ob));
        let (a, b) = company_step.score_of(buffer.bytes(ob));
        score = a;
        total = b;
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, out);
    let (second, again) = hash_artifact(heap, fs, argdir, name);
    var same = false;
    borrow first as &fb in {
        borrow second as &sb in {
            if again == 0 {
                same = bytes.equal(buffer.bytes(fb), buffer.bytes(sb));
            }
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, second);
    if same == false {
        // Whatever the check said, it judged bytes that are not the ones Forge hashed first.
        buffer.drop(heap, first);
        var reason = 5;
        if again != 0 {
            reason = again;
        }
        return refuse_artifact(heap, fs, io, jpath, id, shown, reason, seq);
    }
    var code = company_step.verdict_exit(verdict);
    if verdict == 0 {
        borrow first as &fb in {
            code = record_buffer(heap, fs, io, jpath, company_state.accepted(heap, seq, id, shown, buffer.bytes(fb)), false);
            if code == 0 {
                say(io, "accepted: ", id);
                code = 0 - 1;
            }
        }
    } else if verdict == 1 {
        let failed = record_buffer(heap, fs, io, jpath, company_state.check_failed(heap, seq, id, score, total), false);
        if failed != 0 {
            code = failed;
        } else {
            refuse(io, "check-failed");
            say(io, "step: ", id);
            var sc = buffer.push_nat(heap, buffer.empty(heap, 16), score);
            sc = buffer.push(heap, sc, byte_of(47));
            sc = buffer.push_nat(heap, sc, total);
            borrow sc as &cb in {
                say(io, "score: ", buffer.bytes(cb));
            }
            buffer.drop(heap, sc);
        }
    } else {
        let wrote = record_buffer(heap, fs, io, jpath, company_state.check_unavailable(heap, seq, id, "check-unavailable"), false);
        if wrote != 0 {
            code = wrote;
        } else {
            refuse(io, "check-unavailable");
            say(io, "step: ", id);
            if company_step.is_guard_missing(ran) {
                say(io, "reason: the operator's workers directory has no ", company_step.guard_name());
            }
            if company_step.is_guard_busy(ran) {
                say(io, "reason: another check guard still holds ", company_step.guard_lock());
            }
        }
    }
    buffer.drop(heap, first);
    return code;
}
